554 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 274 0 R/T(23441)/E(23441)/Pg 18 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

555 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 274 0 R/T(Outtake Iterations)/E(Outtake Iterations)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

556 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(The first outtake design used a claw that gripped samples from the inside. However, samples often slipped out when the robot moved, and the inefficient design resulted in fewer game pieces being scored. This version utilized a coaxial virtual four-bar mechanism for claw movement, providing two degrees of freedom: arm rotation and claw rotation. While the claw itself had issues, this mechanism was highly efficient for precise positioning and remained unchanged in later iterations.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

557 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 274 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[986 0 R 0]/K[9]>>
endobj

558 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 274 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[965 0 R 0]/K[10]>>
endobj

559 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 274 0 R/T(CSK Develop3rs)/E(CSK Develop3rs)/Pg 18 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

560 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 274 0 R/T(Page 9)/E(Page 9)/Pg 18 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

561 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(The claw was redesigned to grip samples from the outside, improving precision and making it easier to secure them. This allowed the robot to move faster without the risk of dropping samples, leading to better overall performance. We continued using the coaxial virtual four-bar mechanism from the first version, as it provided smooth and reliable movement with two degrees of freedom, ensuring optimal arm and claw control throughout operation.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

562 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 274 0 R/T(Version 2:)/E(Version 2:)/Pg 18 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

