664 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(The Orca utilizes a hook system to grasp onto the top bars of the submersible, earning additional points during the endgame. Initially, the hooks were designed using 3D-printed filament; however, they lacked the necessary strength to support the robot\220s weight. To address this, we custom-designed and manufactured laser-cut aluminum hooks, significantly improving durability and performance.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

665 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(The sweeper mechanism optimizes sample collection by repositioning pieces for better intake. Without it, the intake would land on top of samples, making collection unreliable. To fix this, we designed a 3D-printed sweeper that rotates a rod under the submersible, allowing the robot to move samples within a 10-inch radius. This prevents interference with the intake system, ensuring smoother operation.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

666 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(Sharing the submersible with our sister team made testing difficult, limiting autonomous performance. To address this, we built a wooden replica matching the real submersible\220s dimensions. This provided additional practice time, allowing us to refine programming and improve consistency. The extra testing helped ensure the robot performed reliably in competition.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

667 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(To create a sleek, durable design, we opted for laser-cut components rather than traditional metal plates. For further personalization, we applied a custom black powder coating by hand, adding both visual appeal and strength. This enhanced the robot\220s durability while maintaining a professional look. The powder coating process helped protect components from wear, ensuring long-lasting performance.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

